11:28:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: # Bullet Trajectory & Hit Detection in FPS Games ## Core Technique: Raycasting Most FPS games don't actually simulate a physical bullet traveling through space. Instead, they use **raycasting** (also called hitscan): 1. The instant you fire, the game draws an invisible mathematical line (ray) from your gun/camera position in the direction you're aiming 2. It checks what that line intersects with first—a wall, a player's hitbox, etc. 3. This calculation happens essentially instantaneously, which is why hitscan weapons (like most pistols/rifles) feel immediate **Projectile-based weapons** (rockets, grenades) work differently—they simulate an actual object moving through the world frame-by-frame, accounting for gravity, travel time, and collision each tick. ## Hitboxes Player models have simplified invisible geometric shapes (capsules, boxes) overlaid on them called **hitboxes**. The raycast checks collision against these simplified shapes rather than the detailed visual mesh, since that's computationally cheaper. ## The Multiplayer/Networking Challenge This is where it gets complex, since players see slightly different game states due to network latency: - **Client-side prediction**: Your own game predicts hits immediately, so shooting feels responsive - **Lag compensation**: The server rewinds time briefly to check where the target *was* on the shooter's screen when they fired, rather than where they are "now" on the server. This is why you sometimes get shot in games after already running behind cover on your own screen - **Server authority**: The server ultimately validates all hits to prevent cheating—clients report shots, but the server confirms damage - **Interpolation/extrapolation**: Game clients smooth out other players' movements between network updates to avoid jerky motion ## Anti-Cheat Considerations Because raycasting relies on trusting client input (aim direction, timing), servers validate: - Line-of-sight (was the target actually visible/unobstructed?) - Plausible fire rates and ammo counts - Reasonable player positions to prevent wallhacks or impossible shots The tradeoff between responsiveness and fairness is one of the most difficult ongoing problems in competitive multiplayer game design—there's no perfect solution, only different compromises.
