11:30:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Great question—this reveals something important about how killcams work, and also that the hitscan/projectile line isn't as clean-cut as I made it seem. ## The Cosmetic Layer vs. The Calculation Layer Even with pure hitscan, games often add a **visual tracer effect** that is purely cosmetic and separate from the actual hit calculation: 1. The hit is resolved instantly (hitscan math determines "yes, this hit the target") 2. *Afterward*, the game draws an animated line/particle effect traveling from gun to target over a short visual duration (maybe 100-200ms) purely for player feedback and immersion 3. This tracer has no bearing on whether the shot hit—the outcome was already decided in step 1 So what you're seeing in a killcam isn't necessarily proof of real trajectory *simulation*—it could just be an animated afterthought layered on top of an already-resolved instant hit. ## How Killcams Actually Work Killcams are typically **replays reconstructed from recorded data**: - The game logs player positions, orientations, and events (like "shot fired," "hit registered") with timestamps - When you die, it plays back a recreation of those recorded moments from the killer's perspective - Since the game already knows the outcome, it can smoothly animate the tracer traveling over that replay window for dramatic effect, even if the underlying hit was resolved instantly in the original calculation ## An Important Caveat I should be honest that I don't have perfect insight into every Call of Duty title's exact implementation (the games are made by different studios—Infinity Ward, Treyarch, Sledgehammer—and mechanics have evolved a lot over the years). Some newer CoD titles have moved toward **actual bullet velocity systems** rather than pure hitscan, especially at longer ranges, partly to make sniper duels and long-range gunfights feel more skill-based. In those cases, the killcam bullet really would reflect genuine travel-time physics, similar to PUBG. So the honest answer is: **it depends on the specific game and weapon**—some killcam bullets are true reconstructions of simulated travel, while others are cosmetic animations dressing up an instantaneous hitscan result. Without digging into that specific game's technical documentation or datamined source info, I can't say with certainty which applies to any single title/weapon combo you're thinking of.
